1 | devops | Close the gap in issue 2745, which you must read in full first before writing anything. THE PROBLEM, verified on main today: go-test in .github/workflows/ci.yml line 220 is path filtered on needs.changes.outputs.go, so a merge touching only apps/web skips the Go suite entirely while the aggregate CI passed check still reports green. Separately, ci-deep.yml runs go-vulncheck and two integration-tagged runs at lines 188 and 237 but never a plain go test over the api module. The consequence, which actually happened: a time-dependent fixture in apps/api/internal/service/recap_confirmation_test.go broke on main when the calendar passed a hardcoded date, survived three consecutive main merges that all reported CI passed, and only surfaced when four dependabot Go PRs went red at once. Fixed in 2744, but the detection gap is still open. YOUR TASK: add one job to .github/workflows/ci-deep.yml that runs the full api module Go unit suite with the race detector against main on the existing nightly schedule. HARD CONSTRAINTS. Do not add anything to ci.yml and do not change the PR gate timing by even one second. The new job must NOT appear in the ci-passed needs list and must NOT appear in the report-main-red needs list, because it must never block a merge or a deploy. It must use the catch-and-file on-failure pattern already present in ci-deep.yml so a failure opens a bug plus ci:deep-failure tracker issue rather than blocking anything. The postgres and redis service blocks must match the go-test job in ci.yml exactly, including env vars and health checks, because a suite that silently skips every database-backed test is worse than no job at all. THE PROOF IS THE POINT: demonstrate the job actually fails when a Go unit test is broken. In a THROWAWAY WORKTREE, commit a deliberate one-line break to a Go unit test, push that throwaway branch, trigger the workflow against it via workflow dispatch, and paste the failing run URL. Commit before you break anything. Never run git stash, which is repo global, and never run git checkout to revert, which zeroes new files unrecoverably. Delete the throwaway branch afterwards. Run make check-ci-wiring and paste the real exit code captured directly rather than read after a pipe, because a pipe returns the exit code of the last command and not the one you are testing. ALSO REPORT BUT DO NOT IMPLEMENT: whether services/hermes and the web unit suite carry the same exposure. Put that finding in the PR body as a recommendation, do not widen the diff to cover them. Update docs/operations/branch-protection.md to record why this job is nightly and deliberately non-blocking. Open a PR with Closes 2745. Omit any Co-Authored-By trailer. Do not merge. | ci/2745-nightly-go-unit-suite

2 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the wave 1 change on branch ci/2745-nightly-go-unit-suite. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. The harness pushes the branch it checks out, so any commit on a producer ref is a production incident. Permitted writes are a slash tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check it at report time, and if it moved report BLOCKED with both SHAs. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: can this new job report success while the Go unit suite is actually broken. A nightly that silently passes is worse than no nightly, because it manufactures false confidence. Enumerate the ways it could pass vacuously rather than trusting the diff: a package filter or run flag that narrows the suite to a subset, a build tag that excludes most tests, a missing service container causing database-backed tests to skip rather than fail, a continue-on-error or an if condition that swallows the result, and a go test invocation whose exit code is consumed by a pipe. SECOND: verify the wave 1 proof genuinely happened. There must be a real failing run URL from a deliberately broken test, not a claim. Reproduce the reasoning and say plainly whether the evidence is real. THIRD: confirm the job cannot block anything. It must be absent from the ci-passed needs list, absent from the report-main-red needs list, and in a workflow that does not gate deploys. Verify by reading both needs lists, not by trusting the PR body. FOURTH: confirm ci.yml is untouched and the PR gate runtime is unchanged. FIFTH: confirm the postgres and redis service definitions match the go-test job in ci.yml, since a drift there is exactly how the suite would pass vacuously. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC 2745 NIGHTLY GO SUITE, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE, per-finding severity and file and line citations. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | ci/2745-nightly-go-unit-suite
